# pi (earendil-works/pi) as a harness in a Shards microVM: the Agentfile kiln ships for it (plan
# 01a1cd8e862e7169a074db3d5fa33f3d, step 28; plan 01a1ce705e62732b83f16295d74ee263, research
# 01a1ce705e62732b86d3534d788ae242, f1-f30). kiln drives pi in its RPC mode (Kind `pi`,
# crates/kiln/src/harness/pi.rs; f13, f14). Its build context is what `kiln agentfiles context pi DIR`
# writes: this file, and for each platform pi-<arch>/ with harness.json, pi's release tarball unpacked
# whole at its root less its pi/ (pi reads its package files beside its executable, f11), and bin/rg
# and bin/fd, each file checked against pin.json. It holds no credential: a person's sign-in stays on
# their own volume (below).

# pi's Linux builds are Bun executables for glibc: they need glibc's loader and link only libc,
# libpthread, libdl and libm, at GLIBC_2.17 at most (f9). Any base of glibc 2.17 or later runs them
# with no RUN; this default, the digest pin.sh read (pin.json's base), is Debian trixie with glibc
# 2.41. pi publishes no musl build, so there is no libc variant, and a musl base (Alpine) can't start
# pi (f10).
ARG BASE=buildpack-deps:trixie-scm@sha256:4f34e1d673fdb65371de9f88bdd32c4e30152fe692b35604aef5c0b3c0e6da10
FROM ${BASE}

# pi's own build for the platform being built (TARGETARCH, f21). Its bin/ holds the ripgrep and fd
# builds pi's grep and find tools would otherwise download, unpinned, at first use: shards-init puts
# it on pi's PATH, where pi finds them, so pi downloads nothing (f16, f17, f18).
ARG TARGETARCH
HARNESS pi FROM ./pi-${TARGETARCH}

# Its network, every part named (default deny; Shards D236, destinations by name): a network for this
# harness alone, which it joins with no flow to any member, and TCP out to the hosts below alone, by
# name, through the network's boundary (--egress) and the microVM's (EXPOSE ... AS egress). Each name
# resolves for it through the agents' resolver, and no other does. Nothing comes in; no other host,
# port or protocol goes out (kiln plan 01a1cd8e862e7169a074db3d5fa33f3d, step 18:
# docs/research/s18-host-in-vm says where each host comes from).
# - pi takes the provider its person configures, so each provider pi 1.5.2's own build carries at a
#   fixed public host is granted (its model catalog's base URLs), with the sign-in hosts of those it
#   signs in to itself (Anthropic, GitHub Copilot, Kimi, Meta, OpenAI, xAI), and pi.dev, its
#   catalog's refreshes (f19). Not granted: a cloud platform's model service, which its own account
#   places (Bedrock, Vertex AI, Azure, Cloudflare); a local server or an endpoint on another port (a
#   local Ollama, f20). Each of those needs an Agentfile of your own, made from this one. Pi takes no
#   MCP server from kiln, and no Agentfile of kiln's grants anything for kiln's MCP.
NETWORK --egress=ai-gateway.vercel.sh:443 --egress=api.ant-ling.com:443 --egress=api.anthropic.com:443 --egress=api.cerebras.ai:443 --egress=api.deepseek.com:443 --egress=api.fireworks.ai:443 --egress=api.github.com:443 --egress=api.groq.com:443 --egress=api.individual.githubcopilot.com:443 --egress=api.kimi.com:443 --egress=api.meta.ai:443 --egress=api.minimax.io:443 --egress=api.minimaxi.com:443 --egress=api.mistral.ai:443 --egress=api.moonshot.ai:443 --egress=api.moonshot.cn:443 --egress=api.openai.com:443 --egress=api.together.ai:443 --egress=api.typesafe.ai:443 --egress=api.x.ai:443 --egress=api.xiaomimimo.com:443 --egress=api.z.ai:443 --egress=auth.kimi.com:443 --egress=auth.meta.com:443 --egress=auth.openai.com:443 --egress=auth.x.ai:443 --egress=chatgpt.com:443 --egress=claude.ai:443 --egress=generativelanguage.googleapis.com:443 --egress=github.com:443 --egress=inference.baseten.co:443 --egress=integrate.api.nvidia.com:443 --egress=open.bigmodel.cn:443 --egress=opencode.ai:443 --egress=openrouter.ai:443 --egress=pi.dev:443 --egress=platform.claude.com:443 --egress=radius.pi.dev:443 --egress=router.huggingface.co:443 --egress=token-plan-ams.xiaomimimo.com:443 --egress=token-plan-cn.xiaomimimo.com:443 --egress=token-plan-sgp.xiaomimimo.com:443 --egress=token-plan.ap-southeast-1.maas.aliyuncs.com:443 --egress=token-plan.cn-beijing.maas.aliyuncs.com:443 provider FOR pi
EXPOSE --harnesses=pi 443/tcp AS egress FOR provider
CONNECT pi WITH pi ON provider

# Its project is plugged in as it starts, at the path it has on the machine (Shards D235), so kiln
# reads its diffs, HEAD and checkpoints at that same path, and no volume holds it. Its HOME is the
# person's volume, whole: harness.json sets HOME=/home/pi, kiln's one rule for every harness it
# ships (step 18). A harness sees its image read-only, with its own /tmp scratch, so a HOME that is
# writable and kept from run to run is a volume, and what the programs it runs keep in a home (a
# .gitconfig, a tool's sign-in) is kept with it. pi keeps its sign-in (auth.json), settings, models
# and sessions in PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR beneath it (f15, f22).
# harness.json keeps caches that follow XDG's rule on the scratch (XDG_CACHE_HOME=/tmp/.cache),
# where they were before its HOME moved onto the volume.
# HOME in run.env needs a shards at or after 997f4e47e (v0.8.0 and later), where run.env replaces
# init's HOME=/tmp. The volume is the harness's alone, and outside /tmp, which the scratch covers.
VOLUME --target-kind=harness /home/pi FOR pi
